# Dialogue

## Purpose

Keep every character sounding like one consistent person across scenes,
sessions, and providers, and keep dialogue audio technically cuttable.

## Scope

Audio layer. Applies to recorded voice, TTS generation (elevenlabs, fal,
local), dialogue editing notes in the edit plan, and voice assets under
`production/generations/` and `references/`.

## Core Principles

- A character's voice is a versioned asset, exactly like their face: defined
  once, approved, and reused — never re-improvised per line.
- The screenplay is the text source of truth; delivered lines match the
  approved script, and deviations flow back through a script revision.
- Every voice asset has provenance (see `common/asset-provenance.md`).

## Voice Identity Per Character

- Every speaking character has a voice profile in their character bible:
  provider and voice ID (or performer), pitch/pace/accent notes, and stability
  settings. The profile is approved before any line is generated and
  versioned `_V##` like any asset.
- All lines for a character MUST use the current approved profile with the
  same settings; changing voice, provider, or settings mid-project is a
  supersession event, and affected lines are regenerated, never mixed.
- Performance direction (emotion, subtext) comes from the scene's beat and
  any parentheticals; direction MUST NOT bend delivery into contradicting
  established character state.
- Aged, disguised, or processed voices are separate named states in the
  profile, mapped to the scenes that use them.

## Recording and TTS Discipline

- One line per file, named by scene, character, and line index; takes are
  attempts under the same generation-record pattern (`GEN_*`), each with
  provider, model/voice ID, text hash, cost, and timestamp.
- Generated dialogue text MUST match the screenplay verbatim; paraphrase is
  a defect even if it sounds better.
- Loudness and format are uniform per project (target loudness, sample rate,
  mono stems) so lines cut together without per-line rescue.
- Room tone / reverb character SHOULD be applied per location at mix, not
  baked inconsistently into individual lines.
- On-camera lines needing lip sync are flagged in the shot file; clips
  carrying them follow `video/clip-boundaries.md` (no mid-speech splits).

## Validation

- `scripts/validate.js` checks each speaking character has an approved voice
  profile before dialogue generation, that line records cite the current
  profile version, and that line text hashes match the screenplay.
- `hooks/require-cost-confirmation.js` gates live TTS batches;
  `hooks/protect-approved-assets.js` protects approved line reads.
- Voice consistency across scenes and performance quality are human review
  by the sound-designer at edit review.
